1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,440
Imagine someone asking you:
"How do you know you're a man?"

2
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:05,760
Or: "How do you know you're a woman?"

3
00:00:06,349 --> 00:00:08,649
You might have to think
about it for a moment.

4
00:00:09,049 --> 00:00:11,629
And then you might answer: "I just know."

5
00:00:11,949 --> 00:00:14,849
And that's actually a
rather interesting answer.

6
00:00:15,449 --> 00:00:18,949
Because most people never have
to examine their gender identity.

7
00:00:19,749 --> 00:00:21,749
Nobody asks them to prove it.

8
00:00:22,229 --> 00:00:25,929
Nobody says: "Explain why
you consider yourself a man."

9
00:00:26,469 --> 00:00:28,329
It's simply part of their life.

10
00:00:28,979 --> 00:00:31,719
But what if that sense
of certainty isn't there?

11
00:00:32,239 --> 00:00:36,439
What if you start wondering: "Am I
really who everyone thinks I am?"

12
00:00:36,607 --> 00:00:40,547
And even more importantly: how
do you know if the answer is no?

13
00:00:42,807 --> 00:00:45,027
There isn't always a single moment.

14
00:00:45,740 --> 00:00:47,880
When we imagine someone
discovering that they're

15
00:00:47,970 --> 00:00:51,380
trans, it's easy to
picture a dramatic moment.

16
00:00:52,044 --> 00:00:55,084
As if one day they suddenly realise: "Oh.

17
00:00:55,324 --> 00:00:57,744
I've actually been
someone else all along."

18
00:00:58,329 --> 00:01:03,089
Sometimes the realisation really can
be that powerful. But not always.

19
00:01:03,629 --> 00:01:06,309
For many people, it's
a much slower process.

20
00:01:07,090 --> 00:01:08,930
It might start with
nothing more than a vague

21
00:01:09,160 --> 00:01:12,190
feeling: "Something about
this doesn't feel right."

22
00:01:12,682 --> 00:01:16,402
But what exactly? Often,
the person doesn't know.

23
00:01:17,047 --> 00:01:19,607
Maybe they don't feel
comfortable in their body.

24
00:01:20,227 --> 00:01:23,227
Maybe they don't like the way
other people address them.

25
00:01:24,098 --> 00:01:27,798
Maybe being seen as a man or a
woman feels strangely uncomfortable.

26
00:01:27,938 --> 00:01:31,018
Or maybe they can't explain it at all.

27
00:01:31,557 --> 00:01:35,037
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

28
00:01:35,657 --> 00:01:37,277
It really helps the channel.

29
00:01:39,447 --> 00:01:41,507
Childhood, puberty, adulthood.

30
00:01:42,423 --> 00:01:46,403
Some trans people say they questioned
their gender from an early age.

31
00:01:47,063 --> 00:01:49,583
Others didn't notice anything like that.

32
00:01:50,180 --> 00:01:52,820
For some, puberty becomes
the turning point.

33
00:01:53,300 --> 00:01:55,580
Their body suddenly starts changing.

34
00:01:55,873 --> 00:01:59,213
And they may discover that changes
which are a completely normal part

35
00:01:59,443 --> 00:02:03,531
of growing up for everyone else
feel deeply uncomfortable to them.

36
00:02:03,611 --> 00:02:06,471
But even then, they may
not think: "I'm trans."

37
00:02:07,051 --> 00:02:09,191
They may not even know
the word transgender.

38
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:13,480
They may simply think: "I hate
what's happening to my body."

39
00:02:14,109 --> 00:02:18,069
And some people don't start asking
these questions until much later.

40
00:02:18,849 --> 00:02:22,109
They may be thirty,
forty, or sixty years old.

41
00:02:22,634 --> 00:02:25,534
They may have a partner,
children, a job, and an entire

42
00:02:25,764 --> 00:02:28,854
life that looks completely
ordinary from the outside.

43
00:02:29,327 --> 00:02:31,627
And yet they may
eventually start wondering:

44
00:02:31,827 --> 00:02:34,147
"Why have I felt this way for so long?"

45
00:02:36,537 --> 00:02:38,397
"But why didn't you know earlier?"

46
00:02:39,215 --> 00:02:41,515
That's actually a reasonable question.

47
00:02:42,275 --> 00:02:45,695
If someone is trans, why didn't
they simply know from the beginning?

48
00:02:46,074 --> 00:02:50,394
But in order to recognise and describe
something, you need words for it.

49
00:02:50,885 --> 00:02:54,345
Imagine someone who has never
heard the word left-handed.

50
00:02:54,725 --> 00:02:58,945
They only know that writing with their
right hand feels strangely difficult.

51
00:02:59,623 --> 00:03:02,823
They might spend years thinking
they're simply bad at writing.

52
00:03:03,344 --> 00:03:07,344
Then one day they discover that some
people naturally use their left hand.

53
00:03:07,604 --> 00:03:10,704
And they think: "Wait. Maybe that's me."

54
00:03:11,250 --> 00:03:14,350
Gender, of course, is not
the same thing as handedness.

55
00:03:14,470 --> 00:03:16,850
But the process of putting
a name to something

56
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:20,030
you've experienced for a
long time can be similar.

57
00:03:22,780 --> 00:03:24,720
"Maybe I'm not man enough."

58
00:03:25,457 --> 00:03:27,077
There's another complication.

59
00:03:27,857 --> 00:03:31,717
Our ideas about being a man or
a woman aren't just about sex.

60
00:03:32,497 --> 00:03:35,297
Society adds a whole
collection of expectations.

61
00:03:36,373 --> 00:03:38,213
Men are supposed to be strong.

62
00:03:38,773 --> 00:03:40,733
Women are supposed to be feminine.

63
00:03:41,442 --> 00:03:43,942
Men are supposed to like certain things.

64
00:03:44,382 --> 00:03:46,362
Women are supposed to like others.

65
00:03:46,765 --> 00:03:49,325
And when someone doesn't fit
these expectations, they may

66
00:03:49,555 --> 00:03:53,025
start wondering: "Maybe I'm
just not masculine enough."

67
00:03:53,205 --> 00:03:56,025
Or: "Maybe I'm not feminine enough."

68
00:03:56,655 --> 00:04:01,175
A trans person can have an additional
question: "Maybe I'm not trans.

69
00:04:01,535 --> 00:04:05,075
Maybe I'm just a man who doesn't
like traditional masculinity."

70
00:04:05,499 --> 00:04:07,499
And that's an important question.

71
00:04:08,199 --> 00:04:12,479
Because not fitting gender stereotypes
is not the same thing as being trans.

72
00:04:13,170 --> 00:04:14,670
A man can love pink.

73
00:04:15,070 --> 00:04:16,610
A woman can hate dresses.

74
00:04:17,130 --> 00:04:19,330
A man can be gentle and sensitive.

75
00:04:20,031 --> 00:04:22,291
A woman can be aggressive,
technically minded,

76
00:04:22,521 --> 00:04:25,291
or completely uninterested
in her appearance.

77
00:04:25,994 --> 00:04:29,374
Gender identity is not a
list of personality traits.

78
00:04:32,034 --> 00:04:33,674
And what about sexuality?

79
00:04:34,733 --> 00:04:37,893
Another source of confusion
can be sexual orientation.

80
00:04:39,013 --> 00:04:42,553
Someone might realise they're
attracted to people of the same sex.

81
00:04:42,943 --> 00:04:45,923
And they might ask: "So
maybe I'm not a man?"

82
00:04:46,363 --> 00:04:50,563
But sexual orientation and gender
identity are two different things.

83
00:04:51,275 --> 00:04:54,295
A man can be gay. A man can be straight.

84
00:04:54,695 --> 00:04:56,295
A man can be bisexual.

85
00:04:56,875 --> 00:04:59,675
And a trans person can have
any sexual orientation.

86
00:05:00,334 --> 00:05:02,334
So being attracted to
certain people doesn't,

87
00:05:02,564 --> 00:05:04,954
by itself, tell you whether you're trans.

88
00:05:07,424 --> 00:05:09,624
"What if I'm just imagining it?"

89
00:05:10,251 --> 00:05:15,911
And now we get to something that may be
much less visible from the outside. Doubt.

90
00:05:16,407 --> 00:05:19,507
Someone may start asking:
"Am I really trans?"

91
00:05:19,707 --> 00:05:22,487
"Or am I just unhappy with my body?"

92
00:05:22,697 --> 00:05:25,397
"Or do I simply hate
traditional gender roles?"

93
00:05:26,037 --> 00:05:29,037
"Or am I trying to explain
something else entirely?"

94
00:05:29,357 --> 00:05:30,937
"What if I'm wrong?"

95
00:05:31,187 --> 00:05:34,127
And sometimes these
questions keep coming back.

96
00:05:34,803 --> 00:05:37,983
There is no simple test that
gives you a result: Trans: yes.

97
00:05:38,213 --> 00:05:39,823
Or: Trans: no.

98
00:05:42,224 --> 00:05:43,664
Suppressing it.

99
00:05:44,290 --> 00:05:47,450
Some people decide they
simply won't think about it.

100
00:05:48,013 --> 00:05:50,633
Maybe they tell themselves:
"This is nonsense."

101
00:05:51,453 --> 00:05:54,533
"I just need to be
normal." "It'll go away."

102
00:05:55,033 --> 00:05:56,993
"I'm not going to deal with this."

103
00:05:57,329 --> 00:06:00,949
And sometimes people continue
living their original lives.

104
00:06:01,628 --> 00:06:05,408
Not everyone who questions their
gender starts transitioning.

105
00:06:06,208 --> 00:06:10,228
And not everyone who discovers that
they're trans decides to make changes.

106
00:06:10,942 --> 00:06:12,562
There can be many reasons.

107
00:06:13,402 --> 00:06:15,522
Someone may not want to change their life.

108
00:06:16,162 --> 00:06:18,502
Someone may not want to change their body.

109
00:06:18,882 --> 00:06:21,262
Someone may not want to tell other people.

110
00:06:21,581 --> 00:06:26,201
And someone may simply reach the
conclusion: "Yes, this is my identity.

111
00:06:26,381 --> 00:06:28,521
But I don't want to transition."

112
00:06:29,402 --> 00:06:31,122
That's not a contradiction.

113
00:06:31,722 --> 00:06:35,562
Identity and what you decide to do
about it are not the same thing.

114
00:06:38,062 --> 00:06:39,842
"So do you have to transition?"

115
00:06:40,825 --> 00:06:43,065
No. And that's an important point.

116
00:06:43,822 --> 00:06:45,482
In the previous video, we distinguished

117
00:06:45,712 --> 00:06:49,182
between social, legal,
and medical transition.

118
00:06:50,042 --> 00:06:53,602
But none of them automatically follows
from discovering that you're trans.

119
00:06:54,352 --> 00:06:56,572
Some people change the way they dress.

120
00:06:57,252 --> 00:06:59,392
Some use a different name or pronouns.

121
00:07:00,274 --> 00:07:03,414
Some take hormones. Some undergo surgery.

122
00:07:03,934 --> 00:07:05,294
Some do only some of these things.

123
00:07:05,524 --> 00:07:07,094
And some do none of them.

124
00:07:07,626 --> 00:07:10,686
Right now, we're interested
in something else: how does

125
00:07:10,916 --> 00:07:13,926
someone actually come to
understand their own identity?

126
00:07:16,616 --> 00:07:18,176
Telling someone else.

127
00:07:18,864 --> 00:07:20,644
And then there may be another step.

128
00:07:20,724 --> 00:07:22,304
Coming out.

129
00:07:23,015 --> 00:07:26,975
Someone who has spent months or
years wondering: "Am I trans?"

130
00:07:27,555 --> 00:07:31,275
may eventually have to tell
someone else: "I think I'm trans."

131
00:07:31,752 --> 00:07:33,552
And that can be a strange moment.

132
00:07:33,632 --> 00:07:36,252
Because inside that person's head,

133
00:07:36,332 --> 00:07:38,372
something may have been
happening for years.

134
00:07:38,761 --> 00:07:42,121
But for the other person, this
may be completely new information.

135
00:07:42,861 --> 00:07:45,061
And they may ask: "How do you know?"

136
00:07:45,343 --> 00:07:47,883
Which brings us right
back to where we started.

137
00:07:49,553 --> 00:07:50,773
How do you know?

138
00:07:51,424 --> 00:07:54,184
Maybe the person doesn't
have a simple answer.

139
00:07:54,844 --> 00:07:57,004
Maybe they can list dozens of memories.

140
00:07:57,837 --> 00:08:00,657
Some from childhood.
Some from adolescence.

141
00:08:01,017 --> 00:08:03,137
Some from the last few months.

142
00:08:03,217 --> 00:08:06,897
But none of them necessarily
proves anything on its own.

143
00:08:07,152 --> 00:08:09,592
And perhaps that's
exactly why the idea of a

144
00:08:09,822 --> 00:08:13,012
universal "trans test"
doesn't really work.

145
00:08:13,227 --> 00:08:15,407
Because gender identity
isn't something most

146
00:08:15,637 --> 00:08:18,007
people discover through
a single measurement.

147
00:08:18,707 --> 00:08:22,067
It's something people come to
understand through their own experience.

148
00:08:25,027 --> 00:08:29,767
There's one more uncomfortable question
we should ask. What if you're wrong?

149
00:08:30,437 --> 00:08:33,817
Some people eventually stop
or change their transition.

150
00:08:34,677 --> 00:08:37,417
This is often described as detransition.

151
00:08:38,087 --> 00:08:39,867
The reasons can be different.

152
00:08:40,687 --> 00:08:43,087
Some people change their gender identity.

153
00:08:43,907 --> 00:08:47,687
Others discover that some parts of
transition aren't right for them.

154
00:08:47,813 --> 00:08:52,293
And for some, the circumstances in which
they live may play an important role.

155
00:08:52,965 --> 00:08:56,165
The existence of detransition
doesn't prove either of

156
00:08:56,395 --> 00:09:00,185
the two simple conclusions
people sometimes draw from it.

157
00:09:00,699 --> 00:09:03,099
It doesn't mean that
everyone who transitions will

158
00:09:03,329 --> 00:09:07,079
eventually discover they were
"really cisgender" all along.

159
00:09:07,719 --> 00:09:12,659
And it doesn't mean that nobody ever
changes their mind or stops transitioning.

160
00:09:13,319 --> 00:09:14,919
People can make mistakes.

161
00:09:15,379 --> 00:09:17,339
People can change their opinions.

162
00:09:17,959 --> 00:09:21,559
And people can understand themselves
differently as they grow older.

163
00:09:22,179 --> 00:09:24,699
That is true far beyond
questions of gender.

164
00:09:27,819 --> 00:09:30,099
So we're back to the original question.

165
00:09:30,679 --> 00:09:32,239
How do you know you're trans?

166
00:09:32,973 --> 00:09:36,733
Some people describe it as something
they knew from a very early age.

167
00:09:37,113 --> 00:09:39,153
Others aren't sure for years.

168
00:09:39,855 --> 00:09:41,475
Some first have to figure out that their

169
00:09:41,705 --> 00:09:44,455
problem isn't simply
with gender stereotypes.

170
00:09:45,135 --> 00:09:48,375
Others don't question their
gender until adulthood.

171
00:09:49,105 --> 00:09:52,625
Some suppress those feelings.
Some don't transition.

172
00:09:53,125 --> 00:09:55,405
And some eventually change their minds.

173
00:09:55,905 --> 00:09:58,005
There isn't one universal story.

174
00:10:00,495 --> 00:10:04,915
Perhaps the most honest answer to the
question: "How do you know you're trans?"

175
00:10:05,490 --> 00:10:07,210
is simply: "Because I tried to understand

176
00:10:07,440 --> 00:10:10,130
why I've felt the way
I've felt for so long."

177
00:10:13,430 --> 00:10:16,210
For one person, the answer
may come very early.

178
00:10:16,450 --> 00:10:18,910
For another, it may take years.

179
00:10:19,150 --> 00:10:23,030
And someone else may never find
a completely certain answer.

180
00:10:23,557 --> 00:10:28,237
But we already know one thing: trans
people don't have to look a certain way.

181
00:10:28,577 --> 00:10:31,217
They don't have to behave a certain way.

182
00:10:31,740 --> 00:10:34,940
They don't have to have a
particular sexual orientation.

183
00:10:35,060 --> 00:10:38,400
And they don't have to undergo
any particular treatment.

184
00:10:38,855 --> 00:10:41,155
But if someone comes to believe
that their body and their

185
00:10:41,385 --> 00:10:45,535
gender identity don't fit
together, another question appears:

186
00:10:45,926 --> 00:10:48,026
what can they actually do about it?

187
00:10:48,486 --> 00:10:50,706
And that's the story we'll explore next.

188
00:10:51,485 --> 00:10:53,715
Why do trans people change their bodies?
